-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Ambiguity:** SMC avoids MC face configuration ambiguities (holes) by using MC-patch logic and treating configurations 11 and 14 as identical [S1].
-*   **Bias:** SMC systematically produces negative volume biases for convex objects and positive for concave objects. The volume bias is larger than MC or Cuberille [S1].
-*   **Accuracy:** Surface error for a discretized sphere converges to 7% for SMC, compared to ~0.4% for MC. Cuberille has a 90% error [S1].
-*   **Normals:** In binarized images, SMC normal RMS error is ~10-12 degrees, comparable to MC. In smooth images, MC is ~4-6 degrees, while SMC remains ~10-12 degrees [S1].
-
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Faster than standard MC. Speedup increases as internal surface area increases [S1].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Half the memory of MC and Cuberille. Vertices use 6 bytes (3 shorts), triangles use 12 bytes (3 longs) [S1].
-*   **Strengths:** Integer arithmetic only, no interpolation, no large lookup table, manifold meshes, efficient for large images and moving boundaries [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Lower accuracy than MC, larger volume bias, higher normal error in smooth fields [S1].
-*   **When to Use:** Simulations of deposition/ablation, porous media diffusivity, where memory and speed are critical and moderate accuracy is acceptable [S1].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When high geometric accuracy or precise normal estimation is required [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Faster than standard MC, with speedup increasing as internal surface area increases [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Uses half the memory of MC and Cuberille. Vertex memory is 6 bytes (3 shorts), triangle memory is 12 bytes (3 longs) [S1].
+*   **Accuracy:** Lower than standard MC but higher than Cuberille. Surface area values lie between Cuberille and MC estimations [S1].
+    *   For a discretized sphere, SMC surface error converges to 7%, while MC converges to ~0.4% [S1].
+    *   Normal RMS error: ~10-12 degrees for binarized images (comparable to MC), ~10-12 degrees for smooth images (MC is ~4-6 degrees) [S1].
+*   **Bias:** SMC systematically produces negative volume biases for convex objects (negative surface bias) and positive surface bias for concave objects. The volume bias is larger than MC or Cuberille [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Integer arithmetic only, no interpolation, guaranteed manifold meshes, lower memory footprint [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Lower accuracy than MC, non-vanishing positive bias in surface estimation for smooth surfaces regardless of sampling rate [S1].
 
 ## Background
-Simplified Marching Cubes was developed for materials science simulations, specifically Chemical Vapor Infiltration (CVI) and ablation modeling [S1]. It was proposed as an efficient alternative to MC for managing physically complex simulations in large 3D blocks [S1]. The method relies on the "MC-patch" configuration list to ensure topological consistency [S1].
+Simplified Marching Cubes was developed for materials science simulations, specifically for computing effective diffusivities in porous media and simulating matrix deposition in Chemical Vapor Infiltration (CVI) and ablation modeling [S1]. It was proposed as an efficient alternative to MC for large-scale simulations where memory and speed are critical, and where the surface consists of triangles composed from vertices of the regular 3D grid [S1]. The method avoids the lookup table and interpolation of standard MC by using cumulative coordinates and node counts to determine triangle connectivity [S1].
 
